-- title:
--   Proposition 3.14: Pointed cones in reductive algebras
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Let $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak g_{\rm compact}\oplus\mathfrak g_p$ be reductive with maximal compact ideal $\mathfrak g_{\rm compact}$, and write $x=x_k+x_p$ accordingly. The following are equivalent:
--
--   $$C_x\text{ pointed};\quad C_{x_p}\text{ pointed and }(x_p\text{ ad-nilpotent},\ x_k\ne0\Rightarrow x_k\notin[\mathfrak g_{\rm compact},\mathfrak g_{\rm compact}]);\quad \operatorname{co}(x)\text{ pointed and }(0\in\operatorname{co}(x)\Rightarrow x_k=0,\ x_p\text{ ad-nilpotent}).$$
--
--   The compact ideal includes the center. This retains every clause of the source criterion.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 11, Proposition 3.14: Pointed cones in reductive algebras

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Frontier30_P26

